<p>President Trump has signaled a retreat from earlier suggestions that the U.S.-Israeli campaign against Iran should lead to regime change, as his public rationale, objectives, and expected timeline for the war have shifted in remarks from him and top officials. Across the coverage, the administration’s stated goals have ranged from encouraging a popular uprising and weakening Tehran’s government to narrower aims such as degrading Iran’s military, security, and nuclear capabilities while reducing its regional influence and supporting Israeli interests. Taken together, the reports suggest the White House is now emphasizing a more limited end state than it did in the opening days of the conflict, even as critics say the shifting explanations make it harder to judge what success would look like or how long U.S. involvement could last. For readers, the main takeaway is that U.S. war aims look less settled than they did at the outset, a sign that policy may still be evolving as the conflict continues.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/rGiSDyld0J13yF9BL3fhvHwsxkeBtmCMj0mbNivgWr4r_C8lKToshvCMALWX2zN1xIDAtKJ9XkmAeQdqtwjQVD3UOmFkSHxx_UNv7smD9w-U-OVQ7H3a3KZfZl2MavIOIxp7NU9BvGfGIvK7T_cGlUbjirLCQjX5cdBhxc1V9bkpVGTJP3yMICcZxbmDC6w-46kKT0NICofwQKHq0MxYDrGOw-S8AUnp5EdxFA' alt='People at an apartment building that was hit by U.S.-Israeli airstrikes in Tehran last week.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Early in the campaign, President Trump pushed for a popular uprising against Tehran’s government, The New York Times reported, before later signaling a step back from that objective.</li><li>Officials described different end goals at different times, according to other coverage, including toppling Iran’s government, limiting its armed capacity, reducing its nuclear capabilities, and shrinking its regional influence.</li><li>The public timeline shifted too, adding to uncertainty about whether Washington expected a short campaign or a longer confrontation.</li><li>Several accounts say support for Israeli interests was part of the administration’s public case for the campaign, alongside U.S. security arguments.</li></ul><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>Trump administration: Senior U.S. officials described the war in shifting terms, at times tying it to pressure for change in Tehran and at other times to narrower military and nuclear goals. (<a href='https://www.thehindu.com/news/international/how-trumps-stated-reasons-goals-and-timeline-for-iran-war-have-shifted/article70767390.ece'>The Hindu</a>)</li><li>President Trump: The president pulled back from earlier calls for an uprising against Iran’s leadership, signaling a narrower public objective than in the campaign’s opening days. (<a href='https://www.nytimes.com/2026/03/20/world/middleeast/trump-winding-down-iran-war.html'>The New York Times</a>)</li><li>Critics: Critics said the changing reasons and timelines pointed to a lack of clarity about the campaign’s purpose and endpoint. (<a href='https://www.ndtv.com/world-news/how-trumps-stated-reasons-goals-and-timeline-for-iran-war-have-shifted-11245573#publisher=newsstand'>NDTV</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.ndtv.com/world-news/how-trumps-stated-reasons-goals-and-timeline-for-iran-war-have-shifted-11245573#publisher=newsstand'>How Trump&#x27;s Stated Reasons, Goals And Timeline For Iran War Have Shifted</a> - ndtv.com</li><li><a href='https://www.thehindu.com/news/international/how-trumps-stated-reasons-goals-and-timeline-for-iran-war-have-shifted/article70767390.ece'>How Trump&#x27;s stated reasons, goals and timeline for Iran war have shifted</a> - thehindu.com</li><li><a href='https://www.nytimes.com/2026/03/20/world/middleeast/trump-winding-down-iran-war.html'>Trump Signals Retreat From Goal of Regime Change in Iran</a> - nytimes.com</li><li><a href='https://www.straitstimes.com/world/middle-east/how-trumps-stated-reasons-goals-and-timeline-for-iran-war-have-shifted'>How Trump’s stated reasons, goals and tim